            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Scientific Studies</strong><br>\nLeonardo's interests were not confined to art. He studied anatomy by dissecting human and animal bodies, which improved his art and contributed to the study of physiology. His scientific notebooks reveal studies of botany, geology, flight, water, and machinery.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Inventions and Machinery</strong><br>\nAlthough many of Leonardo's inventions, such as flying machines and armored vehicles, were never built, they were revolutionary in concept. His sketches of machines are evidence of his foresight into future technological developments.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Notebooks and Writings</strong><br>\nLeonardo's notebooks are a testament to his genius, filled with over 13,000 pages of notes and drawings. They were not published in his lifetime and were only discovered long after, revealing the breadth of his interests and knowledge.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
